-- NOTES:
---- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

-- 1. beneficiary_id is NULL on a payment_transaction row when the
--    transfer is a self-transfer between the customer's own accounts
--    (see the IMPS row above) - no beneficiary record needed for that.
-- 2. The UNIQUE constraint on beneficiary(customer_id, account_number)
--    stops a customer from adding the exact same payee account twice.
--    It does NOT stop two different customers from having the same
--    beneficiary account number - that's expected (they could both be
--    paying the same landlord, for example).
-- 3. transaction_mode drives which real-world rail the transfer used:
--      IMPS - instant, works 24/7, best for small/urgent transfers
--      NEFT - batched, can take longer, no per-transaction cap in India
--      UPI  - uses a VPA (upi_mapping.vpa) instead of account+IFSC
--    Only UPI transactions should reasonably have a UPI-linked account;
--    enforce that pairing in the service layer, not the DB, since a
--    CHECK constraint can't easily cross-reference upi_mapping here.
-- 4. When payment-service (Java) is built, its @Table names must match
--    beneficiary, payment_transaction, upi_mapping exactly, and its
--    enums must match the CHECK constraint values above - same class
--    of integration bug already flagged for account-service.
